    "bounding_box" : null,
    "text" : "This seems like a ligand substitution reaction - two hydroxide ions replacing two water molecules",
    "reading_order" : 39,
    "confidence" : 1.0,
    "role" : "list_item",
    "heading_level" : null,
    "source_engine" : "sme-revision-note",
    "source_engine_version" : "1.0.0"
  }, {
    "element_id" : "e000040",
    "element_type" : "text_block",
    "page_number" : 1,
    "bounding_box" : null,
    "text" : "However this is actually a deprotonation reaction - two hydroxide ions removing hydrogen ions from two of the water ligands converting them into water molecules",
    "reading_order" : 40,
    "confidence" : 1.0,
    "role" : "list_item",
    "heading_level" : null,
    "source_engine" : "sme-revision-note",
    "source_engine_version" : "1.0.0"
  }, {
    "element_id" : "e000041",
    "element_type" : "text_block",
    "page_number" : 1,
    "bounding_box" : null,
    "text" : "The two ligands that have lost hydrogen ions are now hydroxide ligand",
    "reading_order" : 41,
    "confidence" : 1.0,
    "role" : "list_item",
    "heading_level" : null,
    "source_engine" : "sme-revision-note",
    "source_engine_version" : "1.0.0"
  }, {
    "element_id" : "e000042",
    "element_type" : "text_block",
    "page_number" : 1,
    "bounding_box" : null,
    "text" : "Reaction with excess OH<sup>-</sup>",
    "reading_order" : 42,
    "confidence" : 1.0,
    "role" : "heading",
    "heading_level" : 4,
    "source_engine" : "sme-revision-note",
    "source_engine_version" : "1.0.0"
  }, {
    "element_id" : "e000043",
    "element_type" : "text_block",
    "page_number" : 1,
    "bounding_box" : null,
    "text" : "From above, we have seen how hydrated transition metal ions can be deprotonated by adding a base such as aqueous sodium hydroxide to form a metal hydroxide precipitate",
    "reading_order" : 43,
    "confidence" : 1.0,
    "role" : "list_item",
    "heading_level" : null,
    "source_engine" : "sme-revision-note",
    "source_engine_version" : "1.0.0"
  }, {
    "element_id" : "e000044",
    "element_type" : "text_block",
    "page_number" : 1,
    "bounding_box" : null,
    "text" : "For example",
    "reading_order" : 44,
    "confidence" : 1.0,
    "role" : "list_item",
    "heading_level" : null,
    "source_engine" : "sme-revision-note",
    "source_engine_version" : "1.0.0"
  }, {
    "element_id" : "e000045",
    "element_type" : "text_block",
    "page_number" : 1,
    "bounding_box" : null,
    "text" : "**[Cr(H**<sub>**2**</sub>**O)**<sub>**6**</sub>**]**<sup>**3+ **</sup>**(aq) + 3OH**<sup>**- **</sup>**(aq) → [Cr(H**<sub>**2**</sub>**O)**<sub>**3**</sub>**(OH)**<sub>**3**</sub>**] (s) +3H**<sub>**2**</sub>**O (l) **",
    "reading_order" : 45,
    "confidence" : 1.0,
    "role" : "paragraph",
    "heading_level" : null,
    "source_engine" : "sme-revision-note",
    "source_engine_version" : "1.0.0"
  }, {
    "element_id" : "e000046",
    "element_type" : "text_block",
    "page_number" : 1,
    "bounding_box" : null,
    "text" : "When an excess of sodium hydroxide is added further deprotonation takes place",
    "reading_order" : 46,
    "confidence" : 1.0,
    "role" : "list_item",
    "heading_level" : null,
    "source_engine" : "sme-revision-note",
    "source_engine_version" : "1.0.0"
  }, {
    "element_id" : "e000047",
    "element_type" : "text_block",
    "page_number" : 1,
    "bounding_box" : null,
    "text" : "**[Cr(H**<sub>**2**</sub>**O)**<sub>**3**</sub>**(OH)**<sub>**3**</sub>**] (s) + 3OH**<sup>**-**</sup>** (aq) → [Cr(OH)**<sub>**6**</sub>**]**<sup>**3-**</sup>** (aq) + 3H**<sub>**2**</sub>**O (l) **",
    "reading_order" : 47,
    "confidence" : 1.0,
    "role" : "paragraph",
    "heading_level" : null,
    "source_engine" : "sme-revision-note",
    "source_engine_version" : "1.0.0"
  }, {
    "element_id" : "e000048",
    "element_type" : "text_block",
    "page_number" : 1,
    "bounding_box" : null,
    "text" : "In this reaction, chromium(III) hydroxide acts as an acid, as it is reacting with a base",
    "reading_order" : 48,
    "confidence" : 1.0,
    "role" : "list_item",
    "heading_level" : null,
    "source_engine" : "sme-revision-note",
    "source_engine_version" : "1.0.0"
  }, {
    "element_id" : "e000049",
    "element_type" : "text_block",
    "page_number" : 1,
    "bounding_box" : null,
    "text" : "Chromium(III) hydroxide can also act as a base because it can react with acids as follows",
    "reading_order" : 49,
    "confidence" : 1.0,
    "role" : "list_item",
    "heading_level" : null,
    "source_engine" : "sme-revision-note",
    "source_engine_version" : "1.0.0"
  }, {
    "element_id" : "e000050",
    "element_type" : "text_block",
    "page_number" : 1,
    "bounding_box" : null,
    "text" : "**[Cr(H**<sub>**2**</sub>**O)**<sub>**3**</sub>**(OH)**<sub>**3**</sub>**] (s) + 3H**<sup>**+ **</sup>**(aq) → [Cr(H**<sub>**2**</sub>**O)**<sub>**6**</sub>**]**<sup>**3+**</sup>** (aq) **",
    "reading_order" : 50,
    "confidence" : 1.0,
    "role" : "paragraph",
    "heading_level" : null,
    "source_engine" : "sme-revision-note",
    "source_engine_version" : "1.0.0"
  }, {
    "element_id" : "e000051",
    "element_type" : "text_block",
    "page_number" : 1,
    "bounding_box" : null,
    "text" : "A metal hydroxide that can act as both an acid and a base is called an amphoteric hydroxide",
    "reading_order" : 51,
    "confidence" : 1.0,
    "role" : "list_item",
    "heading_level" : null,
    "source_engine" : "sme-revision-note",
    "source_engine_version" : "1.0.0"
  }, {
    "element_id" : "e000052",
    "element_type" : "text_block",
    "page_number" : 1,
    "bounding_box" : null,
    "text" : "This is an example of amphoteric behaviour",
    "reading_order" : 52,
    "confidence" : 1.0,
    "role" : "list_item",
    "heading_level" : null,
    "source_engine" : "sme-revision-note",
    "source_engine_version" : "1.0.0"
  }, {
    "element_id" : "e000053",
    "element_type" : "text_block",
    "page_number" : 1,
    "bounding_box" : null,
    "text" : "Reaction with excess NH<sub>3</sub>",
    "reading_order" : 53,
    "confidence" : 1.0,
    "role" : "heading",
    "heading_level" : 4,
    "source_engine" : "sme-revision-note",
    "source_engine_version" : "1.0.0"
  }, {
    "element_id" : "e000054",
    "element_type" : "text_block",
    "page_number" : 1,
    "bounding_box" : null,
    "text" : "With excess NH<sub>3</sub> ligand substitution reactions occur with Cu, Co and Cr and their precipitates dissolve",
    "reading_order" : 54,
    "confidence" : 1.0,
    "role" : "list_item",
    "heading_level" : null,
    "source_engine" : "sme-revision-note",
    "source_engine_version" : "1.0.0"
  }, {
    "element_id" : "e000055",
    "element_type" : "text_block",
    "page_number" : 1,
    "bounding_box" : null,
    "text" : "The ligands NH<sub>3</sub> and H<sub>2</sub>O are similar in size and are uncharged",
    "reading_order" : 55,
    "confidence" : 1.0,
    "role" : "list_item",
    "heading_level" : null,
    "source_engine" : "sme-revision-note",
    "source_engine_version" : "1.0.0"
  }, {
    "element_id" : "e000056",
    "element_type" : "text_block",
    "page_number" : 1,
    "bounding_box" : null,
    "text" : "Ligand exchange occurs without a change of co-ordination number for Co and Cr",
    "reading_order" : 56,
    "confidence" : 1.0,
    "role" : "list_item",
    "heading_level" : null,
    "source_engine" : "sme-revision-note",
    "source_engine_version" : "1.0.0"
  }, {
    "element_id" : "e000057",
    "element_type" : "text_block",
    "page_number" : 1,
    "bounding_box" : null,
    "text" : "For example when excess aqueous ammonia is added to a copper(II) hydroxide precipitate it dissolves forming a deep blue solution",
    "reading_order" : 57,
    "confidence" : 1.0,
    "role" : "list_item",
    "heading_level" : null,
    "source_engine" : "sme-revision-note",
    "source_engine_version" : "1.0.0"
  }, {
    "element_id" : "e000058",
    "element_type" : "text_block",
    "page_number" : 1,
    "bounding_box" : null,
    "text" : "**[Cu(H**<sub>**2**</sub>**O)**<sub>**4**</sub>**(OH)**<sub>**2**</sub>**] (s) + 4NH**<sub>**3**</sub>** (aq) → [Cu(NH**<sub>**3**</sub>**)**<sub>**4**</sub>**(H**<sub>**2**</sub>**O)**<sub>**2**</sub>**]**<sup>**2+ **</sup>**(aq) + 2H**<sub>**2**</sub>**O (l) + 2OH**<sup>**-**</sup>** (aq)**",
    "reading_order" : 58,
    "confidence" : 1.0,
    "role" : "paragraph",
    "heading_level" : null,
    "source_engine" : "sme-revision-note",
    "source_engine_version" : "1.0.0"
  }, {
    "element_id" : "e000059",
    "element_type" : "text_block",
    "page_number" : 1,
    "bounding_box" : null,
    "text" : "This is a ligand substitution - four ammonia molecules replace two water molecules and two hydroxide ions",
    "reading_order" : 59,
    "confidence" : 1.0,
    "role" : "list_item",
    "heading_level" : null,
    "source_engine" : "sme-revision-note",
    "source_engine_version" : "1.0.0"
  }, {
    "element_id" : "e000060",
    "element_type" : "text_block",
    "page_number" : 1,
    "bounding_box" : null,
    "text" : "In these reactions NH<sub>3</sub> is acting as a Lewis base donating an electron pair",
